**Short answer: no major contribution.** By "major" I mean a new method, a long-standing or prominent problem resolved, a better bound than the current state of the art, or a physical result. None of the five clears that bar. What we have are short answers to recent, explicitly posed questions in a small specialist literature, built mostly from known tools. If they survive human review and priority checks, two are genuine specialist contributions (forest, universality), one is a companion construction, one is mostly a corollary of 2014 work, and one is a narrow no-go.

| Result | State of the art before us | What we add | Calibrated value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Spiral dense forest | Open. Adiceam–Tsokanos (2022) conjectured that **no** spiral dense forest exists; Tevet (2025) conjectured one does and proved no constant-angle Fermat spiral is one; a 2025 Toronto PhD thesis still calls existence unknown. Lattice-union forests are known (Shirandami 2023). | First example found: a norm-ranked union of two rotated lattices with exact radii √k. Settles the planar question; refutes the 2022 conjecture. | Strongest of the five, but short: Tevet's own Prop. 6 and his suggested route make the transfer step routine. No visibility rate (best known ≈ ε⁻¹ log ε⁻¹; optimal ε⁻¹ is equivalent to Danzer's problem, open). Modest specialist result. |
| Lattice universality | Tevet (2025) characterized the limits for one fixed badly approximable angle and asked whether every covolume-π lattice occurs. Local lattices and area π: Bacher (2014). Winning sets: McMullen. | Yes: each target lattice, with all rotations and translates, arises from one bounded-digit angle. The angle must depend on the target; that is provably necessary. | Complete answer to an explicit question, with one non-elementary published input. A dynamics specialist may see it as known bounded-geodesic facts translated through Bacher's picture [inference]. Modest-to-substantive specialist result. |
| Higher-dimensional spiral density | Adiceam–Tsokanos (2020/21) asked whether, for n ≥ 3, a spiral Delone set can have asymptotic density; never restated or answered since. n = 2 known. | Yes, for every n ≥ 2, by norm-ranking ℤⁿ. | Answers the literal question, but every ingredient is classical (Landau–Hlawka counts, "well known" equidistribution of lattice directions, density under bounded displacement). Corollary-level; same mechanism as the forest. |
| Fermat limit classification | Tevet (2025) conjectured the types ∅, ℝ, ℤ², ℤ×ℝ for non-badly-approximable angles. Bacher (2014), uncited by Tevet, already gives local lattices of area π. | Nonempty limits are only lines and covolume-π lattices; ℤ×ℝ and ℝ² never occur, correcting Tevet's computational suggestion. | Mostly a corollary of Bacher (2014). Supporting proposition. |
| Supersoliton obstruction | Wang–Tang–Liang (2018) hypothesized integrability for all n and suggested a three-super-soliton test without fixing an ansatz. The 2026 classification (Delisle–Jaouadi) covers single-tau equations only. | Our chosen generic mixed-seed test fails for every n ≥ 4. | Narrow and interpretation-dependent. The super part equals our earlier bosonic result plus the standard fermionic-limit reduction the 2018 paper itself uses. Symmetry integrability untouched. |

None of the four phyllotaxis results says anything about the book's physics (least-energy Fibonacci patterns on cones). Physical work there is numerical ground-state and defect modeling, e.g. repulsive particles on cones and horns (Ansell–Tomlinson–Wilkin 2020); ours are geometric existence theorems, a different kind of claim.

Best honest framing: two coherent items plus a note. (A) Norm-ranking spiral constructions (forest + density): one shared mechanism, so one shared priority risk. (B) Chabauty limits of Fermat spirals (universality, with classification as a proposition crediting Bacher first). (C) A scoped supersoliton no-go.

What would count as major here: a spiral forest with an explicit near-optimal visibility rate, or a Danzer spiral (Tevet's follow-up questions); a Delone spiral forest with an explicit rate; a dynamically natural spiral with density for n ≥ 3 (Akiyama's question); a classification of supersymmetric mKdV-type bilinear equations (left open by Delisle–Jaouadi); or a theorem about actual energy minimizers on cones.

Next steps, in order of value:
1. Human specialist review of forest + density together, with their shared priority check: Adiceam's 2023 HDR, the journal text of the 2022 density paper, Berestovskii (2005). The same method likely extends the forest to every dimension, which would refute the 2022 conjecture outright [inference, unproved].
2. Universality: ask a homogeneous-dynamics specialist whether "bounded geodesic through any point + continued-fraction concatenation" is folklore. Read the 2025 *Mathematical Phyllotaxis* book and Tevet's journal version (both paywalled; needs library access).
3. Fold classification into the universality write-up. Keep the soliton note scoped, and check Hietarinta's 1990 chapter, which may already cover n = 5.

Limits: only AI models have checked the proofs (two vendors for forest and universality, one for the others); no human review. Publisher texts of Tevet, Adiceam–Tsokanos 2022 and the 2022 density paper are paywalled. A failed search does not establish priority.

