evidence/SolitonSota.md:5: "summary": "Fresh SOTA audit, 2026-10-14. (1) The super theorem is really a bosonic theorem. Our projection is the solution-level form of the standard fermionic/bosonic-limit reduction, which Wang–Tang–Liang 2018 apply themselves for n=2,3 and Delisle–Jaouadi 2026 use for single-tau equations. The restricted lift F=f(x,y+θζ,t) gives the converse. So for n≥4 the result is equivalent to the workspace's earlier bosonic obstruction (C4) for (D_t+D_x^n)F·G=0, D_y(D_t+D_x^n)F·G=0. (2) No source settles that bosonic statement for all n. Hietarinta 1987 II only searched pairs whose second polynomial is quadratic (zbMATH review, confirmed by Zhang–Liu–Zhang 2025). His later finite search (1990 chapter, summarized in 1997) lists five mKdV-type pairs; Eq. (37) at a=0 is our n=3 member. Delisle–Jaouadi arXiv:2609.14372v1 is still the only version and still the newest relevant arXiv paper; it classifies single-tau, odd-P, (1+1) equations and leaves supersymmetric mKdV-type equations open. (3) So the all-n super exclusion follows from our own bosonic theorem plus a standard projection, not from any published classification. One unverified exception: n=5 might fall inside Hietarinta's 1990 search. (4) Proofs that the three-soliton test fails for all degrees are a known genre (Guo 1991 via Ma 2021; Gürses–Pekcan 2025). (5) The 2018 hypothesis is explicit but imprecise; the generic quantifier and the F/G seed basis are our choices. For odd n our criterion is stronger than Hietarinta's literal test (only the b_i=±a_i sectors). The results agree with the conditional Li–Lou/book ansatz; they are not a counterexample to it.",
evidence/SolitonSota.md:12:  {"claim":"Delisle–Jaouadi treat one even superfield τ(x,t,θ). The S direction is the polynomial variable X, and P must be odd (Eq. 6).","quote":"We assume that the polynomial 𝒫 is odd i.e. 𝒫(−X,−T)=−𝒫(X,T).","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle & Jaouadi, arXiv:2609.14372v1, §II","date":"2026-09-13 (v1; /abs/2609.14372v2 returned HTTP 404 on 2026-10-14)","kind":"primary preprint (arXiv HTML)","verified":true,"stance":"nuance","scope":"single-tau, odd P, (1+1)"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:13:  {"claim":"Delisle–Jaouadi recover the bosonic three-soliton condition E=0 (Hietarinta's) as the bosonic limit. Projecting a super condition to its body is standard in this literature.","quote":"We retrieve, in the bosonic limit, Hietarinta’s condition for classical KdV-type equations.","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle–Jaouadi v1, §I; E=0 in §IV","date":"2026-09-13","kind":"primary preprint","verified":true,"stance":"counter","scope":"novelty of the projection step"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:14:  {"claim":"In Delisle–Jaouadi the obstruction is fermionic: P=aX^5+bX^3+cX+T satisfies the bosonic E=0 condition, yet the fermionic conditions force a=0 (Eqs. 42–44). Our obstruction works the other way: it is purely bosonic.","quote":"We show that, for free fermionic parameters ξ1, ξ2 and ξ3, these conditions impose a=0 for the polynomial 𝒫.","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle–Jaouadi v1, §V, Eqs. (42)–(44)","date":"2026-09-13","kind":"primary preprint","verified":true,"stance":"nuance","scope":"single-tau quintic family"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:15:  {"claim":"Their 'strong' integrability means unconstrained parameters apart from the dispersion relations. This supports our generic, unconstrained-parameter criterion as the standard meaning in super-Hirota work.","quote":"strongly Hirota integrable if and only if the eight integrability conditions are satisfied without any constraints on the bosonic and fermionic parameters apart from the dispersion relations.","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle–Jaouadi v1, §V","date":"2026-09-13","kind":"primary preprint (definition)","verified":true,"stance":"supports","scope":"definition of the criterion"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:16:  {"claim":"Their (2+1) example Eq. (50), S(D_t+D_y^3)τ·τ=0, comes from Delisle–Mosaddeghi 2013 (also ref [4] in the 2018 paper), not from Wang–Tang–Liang. It passes all eight conditions. After swapping x and y it is our F=G, n=3 sector, which our theorem does not exclude.","quote":"In [35], the authors showed that an unconstrained two-soliton solution existed, but didn’t proposed an unconstrained three super-soliton solution.","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle–Jaouadi v1, §V, Eq. (50); ref [35] = Delisle & Mosaddeghi, J. Phys. A 46, 115203 (2013)","date":"2026-09-13","kind":"primary preprint","verified":true,"stance":"nuance","scope":"F=G, n=3 overlap"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:17:  {"claim":"Delisle–Jaouadi leave supersymmetric mKdV-type equations open. Their reference list has no entry for Wang–Tang–Liang 2018 or Li–Lou 2008. No 2026 source settles the paired family.","quote":"Can the present classification be generalized to supersymmetric mKdV-, Sine-Gordon- and complex-type bilinear equations, where different algebraic structures are expected to emerge ?","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14372v1","source":"Delisle–Jaouadi v1, §VI; reference list [1]–[47]","date":"2026-09-13","kind":"primary preprint","verified":true,"stance":"supports","scope":"whether a 2026 source settles the question"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:24:  {"claim":"All-degree failure of the three-soliton test was already proved for a single-tau family. This is the same kind of result as our bosonic proof.","quote":"It has also been proved [14] that the higher-order KdV equations associated with P1(x,t) = x^{2n} + xt, n ≥ 4, (3.26) does not pass the three-soliton test.","url":"http://shell.cas.usf.edu/~wma3/Ma-IJNSNS2021.pdf","source":"W.-X. Ma, IJNSNS (2021), §3.2.2, doi:10.1515/ijnsns-2020-0214; [14] = Guo F.K., Acta Math. Appl. Sin. 14 (1991) 111–114","date":"2021 (Guo 1991)","kind":"primary paper citing a 1991 result","verified":"partial: Ma's statement read; Guo 1991 not accessed","stance":"counter","scope":"novelty of the method"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:25:  {"claim":"Some high-degree single-tau families pass the three-soliton test for every k. Failure at degree ≥4 depends on the structure; it is not a general law.","quote":"We show that the equations for P(D)=D_x(D_x^3+α_1 D_t+α_2 D_y)^{2k+1} possess three-soliton solutions for any constants (α_1,α_2)≠(0,0) and k∈ℕ.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.18466","source":"Gürses & Pekcan, 'Higher order Hirota bilinear forms', abstract","date":"2025-11-23","kind":"primary preprint (abstract only)","verified":true,"stance":"nuance","scope":"all-degree tests"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:29:  {"claim":"The bosonic F=G, n=5 sector has 3- and 4-soliton solutions only under wave-number constraints. This fits generic failure, and the sector is outside our theorem.","quote":"The existence of three-soliton and four-soliton solutions with constraints on wave numbers is confirmed.","url":"https://www.researchsquare.com/article/rs-3801786/latest.pdf","source":"Wang, Yang, Tang, Chen, Nonlinear Dyn. (2024), doi:10.1007/s11071-024-09511-0; preprint abstract","date":"2024-04-09","kind":"primary paper (abstract); grep found no mention of the 2018 hypothesis or supersymmetry","verified":true,"stance":"supports","scope":"F=G, n=5"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:30:  {"claim":"No newer contrary evidence found. The 2024 paper's 5 OpenAlex citers (2024–2026) are about other equations. An arXiv API search sorted by date (Hirota AND supersymmetric/three-soliton) has 2609.14372v1 as the newest hit. Crossref shows 0 citers of the 2018 paper (index dated 2024-04-19).","quote":"","url":"https://api.openalex.org/works?filter=cites:W4394615218","source":"OpenAlex, arXiv API, Crossref","date":"queried 2026-10-14","kind":"metadata","verified":true,"stance":"supports","scope":"forward screen (counts do not prove openness or significance)"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:33:  {"claim":"The 2026 review of the bilinear method only summarizes Hietarinta's searches. It gives no classification for paired R·P systems.","quote":"Later, he extended the search to the bilinear equations of the modified KdV (mKdV) type and sine-Gordon type under the conditions of having 3SSs","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2606.16205v1","source":"Da-jun Zhang, 'Integrability and transformations in the bilinear method: An introduction', §1 (OCNMP special issue for Hietarinta)","date":"2026-06-15","kind":"primary review","verified":true,"stance":"nuance","scope":"background"},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:38:  {"baseline":"Delisle–Jaouadi arXiv:2609.14372v1 (§II–V)","baseline_assumptions":"One even superfield τ(x,t,θ); S built from D=∂θ+θ∂x; P(X,T) odd, (1+1); dressed three-super-soliton ansatz with free odd parameters ξ_i","baseline_conclusion":"Eight conditions; the bosonic one (E=0) is Hietarinta's. aX^5+bX^3+cX+T passes without constraints only if a=0. SUSY Sawada–Kotera–Ramani is only weakly integrable (needs Carstea's relation, Eq. 46). The (2+1) super-BLMP equation, Eq. 50, passes all eight.","ours_assumptions":"Two independent even F,G on (x,y,t,θ); S_y with D_θ=∂θ+θ∂y (the S direction is not the dispersion variable); P_n=D_t+D_x^n, odd or mixed parity; any square-free body with arbitrary nilpotent dressings; generic F–F–G seeds","ours_conclusion":"No such family for any n≥4. The obstruction is in the body.","exact_delta":"The hypotheses do not overlap except at the closed odd-n F=G sector, which our theorem does not exclude (at n=3 it is Eq. 50 and passes). Their obstruction is fermionic with E=0 holding; ours is bosonic. Neither result implies the other."},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:40:  {"baseline":"Hietarinta 1997 §4.2 (summarizing 1987 II/III and the 1990 chapter)","baseline_assumptions":"Form (32): A even, B odd; soliton kinds as in (33); 'any set of three solitons'; finite degree search","baseline_conclusion":"Five mKdV-type pairs, Eqs. (34)–(38); (37) at a=0 equals our n=3","ours_assumptions":"F/G seeds; for odd n, independent two-amplitude seeds (mixing the two kinds); even n included","ours_conclusion":"Fails for every n≥4","exact_delta":"Covers all degrees and even n. For odd n our criterion is stronger than his pure-kind test (the b_i=±a_i sectors are not covered). n=5 may sit inside his search bounds, which are not given."},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:43:  {"baseline":"Wang–Yang–Tang–Chen 2024","baseline_assumptions":"Bosonic F=G, n=5","baseline_conclusion":"3- and 4-soliton solutions only with wave-number constraints","ours_assumptions":"F≠G, generic","ours_conclusion":"Fails","exact_delta":"Complementary sectors; consistent with each other."},
evidence/SolitonSota.md:51:  "what_would_raise_it":["Prove the literal Hietarinta pure-kind test fails for all odd n≥5","Settle the F=G single-tau sectors for odd n≥5, including the fermionic conditions","Classify paired P=D_t+p(D_x) systems for arbitrary multipliers R(D_y), or the supersymmetric mKdV-type class Delisle–Jaouadi leave open"]
