evidence/DensitySota.md:3:  "summary": "I found no later source that settles the exact Adiceam–Tsokanos (AT) question. I also found no earlier source with the same construction, which takes the nonzero integer lattice ordered by length and sets s_k = k^(1/n)·z_k/|z_k|. Every such source I could inspect was checked. This is a bounded screen, not proof of novelty. Of the public versions I could inspect, only arXiv v1 (13 Oct 2020) and v3 (30 Mar 2021, still the latest) state the question. v3 is §6, pp.22–23. I could not read the journal text. Indirect metadata suggests §6 survived into the journal version; this is inference. Tsokanos's 2022 thesis says it includes the referee revisions, but its Chapter 4 leaves out §5 and §6. The authors' later papers through Oct 2026 do not repeat the question. The new prior-art screen found related constructions but no equivalent. A 2010 MathOverflow answer in the discussion AT cite as [10] floated numbering lattice points shell by shell, informally and with no radius rescaling. Graiff Zurita–Kane–Oishi-Tomiyasu (Constructive Approximation 2024) built a local '3D Vogel spiral' from a lattice and a volume-preserving map, and left the global problem open. Lodkin (2019) gave a multidimensional phyllotaxis model with conjectures only. Every analytic ingredient of our proof is classical. The lattice-ball error bound is due to Landau and Hlawka. Uniform distribution of lattice directions is called 'well known' in Marklof–Strömbergsson (2010), eq. (2.8). Bounded displacement preserving density is standard (Laczkovich 1992; Frettlöh–Smilansky–Solomon, Cor. 3.2). The result therefore answers a stated, narrow specialist question with a short combination of standard lemmas. I rate it a corollary-level modest extension, not a substantive specialist advance and not a breakthrough. It also leaves the motivating questions open: whether a single-orbit (dynamical) construction exists, and whether the directions can be pairwise distinct.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:6:      "arXiv:2010.06428v3 (30 Mar 2021), §6 pp.22–23. Still the latest version: the listing shows v1 13 Oct 2020, v2 14 Oct 2020, v3 30 Mar 2021 12:51:59 UTC.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:7:      "arXiv:2010.06428v1 (13 Oct 2020), §6 has the same sentence. The question was present from the first version."
evidence/DensitySota.md:9:    "journal_version": "Funct. Approx. Comment. Math. 67(1):21–46, published 2022-09-14, DOI 10.7169/facm/1958. Text not read: Project Euclid sits behind Incapsula bot protection and was not retried, per instructions. Crossref (references deposited 2026-10-07) lists the same 10 references as v3. These include [5] Drmota–Tichy, which v3 cites only once, inside §6 on p.22 ('see [5, §1.1.3]'). [INFERENCE] §6 was very likely kept. Whether the density paragraph itself was kept is unverified.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:10:    "later_author_sources": "None of these restates the question. The 2022 Tsokanos thesis, which says it 'takes into account the revisions … following referees reports', omits §5 and §6. The visibility paper (v2, 2022) calls the higher-dimensional Delone question 'settled' and does not mention density. The Danzer survey v4 has no 'spiral' at all. HDR 2023: HAL's full-text index has 'spiral' and 'Delone' but no 'asymptotic density' or 'densité asymptotique' [index-level INFERENCE]. Adiceam and Tsokanos arXiv papers from 2024 to Oct 2026 are on unrelated topics.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:14:    {"claim": "Exact open question (AT v3)", "quote": "It is not clear to the authors whether a spiral Delone set with asymptotic density r = V_n^{-1} can exists in any dimension n ≥ 3.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "Adiceam–Tsokanos, Higher Dimensional Spiral Delone Sets, §6 p.23", "date": "2021-03-30 (v3)", "kind": "primary preprint (later refereed)", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "Existence for n≥3, exact radii k^(1/n), density for every bounded Jordan set. Superscript-inverse normalization confirmed from the package's rendered page images."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:15:    {"claim": "The question was already in v1", "quote": "It is not clear to the authors whether a spiral Delone set with asymptotic density r = V_n−1 can exists in any dimension n ≥ 3.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v1", "source": "AT arXiv v1, §6", "date": "2020-10-13", "kind": "primary preprint", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "Unchanged across v1 to v3. The 'V_n−1' is an OCR artifact of V_n^{-1}."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:16:    {"claim": "AT's own reduction: density holds iff the directions are uniformly distributed on caps", "quote": "It follows from an elementary adaptation of the argument presented in [6, Proposition 1] that the spiral set defined in (1.3) has asymptotic density r = V_n^{-1} … if, and only if, … (u_k) … is uniformly distributed", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "AT v3 §6 p.22", "date": "2021-03-30", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "So the density half of the question reduces to uniform distribution of the directions. That is classical for lattice directions (see Marklof–Strömbergsson below)."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:17:    {"claim": "Arbitrary, non-dynamical directions are explicitly in scope", "quote": "the set S_x need not resemble a spiral.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "AT v3 p.2 footnote 1", "date": "2021-03-30", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "Definition (1.3) imposes no orbit, continuity or distinct-direction requirement."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:18:    {"claim": "AT deliberately chose not to use orbits of transitive maps", "quote": "Akiyama [1] suggests that the sequence (u_k) might be chosen as the orbit of a transitive map … We adopt here a rather different approach in order to define our spherical sequence.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "AT v3 p.4", "date": "2021-03-30", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "AT use a lifted Kronecker sequence ({kα}, α badly approximable) with stereographic maps and an even/odd split. That construction is Delone. [INFERENCE] It has no density, because the lifts do not preserve measure: the even- and odd-indexed points spread out according to the distorted measure, not the uniform one."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:19:    {"claim": "Fixed-angle/dynamical program (Akiyama)", "quote": "One possibility is to find a transitive action T on S^{d−1}, so that {n^{1/d} T^n(x0) | n ∈ N} is a Delone set in R^d.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.10815v2", "source": "Akiyama, Spiral Delone sets and three distance theorem, §4 Open Questions, p.8", "date": "2019-04-30 (v2); Nonlinearity 33(5) 2020", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Our construction is not an orbit. This dynamical question remains open."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:20:    {"claim": "Journal reference list matches v3, including a reference cited only in §6", "quote": "[5] M. Drmota, and R. Tichy, Sequences, discrepancies and applications, Lecture Notes in Mathematics, 1651, Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 1997.", "url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.7169/facm/1958", "source": "Crossref metadata for FACM 67(1):21–46", "date": "published 2022-09-14; deposited 2026-10-07", "kind": "bibliographic metadata", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "[INFERENCE] §6 retained in the journal. The density paragraph itself is not verified. Crossref is-referenced-by-count is 1."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:22:    {"claim": "The authors' later framing calls the Delone question settled and does not mention density", "quote": "The resulting question of determining whether this theory can be extended to higher dimensions was later settled by the authors", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.01843v2", "source": "Adiceam–Tsokanos, Visibility Properties of Spiral Sets, introduction", "date": "2022-06-03", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Its open questions are about dense forests. It does not restate the density question."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:23:    {"claim": "Problem origin, cited by AT as [10]", "quote": "we want to put a sequence of nodes down in R^3 so that the kth node is at radius ∛k from the origin, and so that the resulting pattern is reasonably well packed", "url": "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/24850", "source": "MathOverflow 24850 (H. Segerman)", "date": "2010-05-16", "kind": "discussion forum", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Informal. Six answers, none accepted."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:24:    {"claim": "Closest informal precursor: number lattice spheres shell by shell", "quote": "perhaps somehow number the spheres used to form Waterman polyhedra? That gives a much more regular and close-packed set of points (sphere centers) -- but it's not obvious to me if there is any nice formula", "url": "https://mathoverflow.net/a/30142", "source": "MO 24850 answer by user7234 (read via the Stack Exchange API)", "date": "2010-07-01", "kind": "discussion forum", "verified": true, "stance": "counter", "scope": "Suggests ordering fcc lattice points by shells. It does not rescale to radius ∛k, does not consider the radial correction, and proves nothing. Not an equivalent, but it shows the idea was close at hand."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:25:    {"claim": "Dynamical suggestion on MO", "quote": "you can take the orbit of a point under the action, ordering by something like word length.", "url": "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/24850", "source": "MO 24850 answer by S. Carnahan (free-group action on S^2)", "date": "2010-05-16", "kind": "discussion forum", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Orbit or word-length ordering, with no packing or density result."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:26:    {"claim": "Earlier higher-dimensional golden-angle spiral (lattice plus volume-preserving map)", "quote": "packings in a plane with logarithmic spirals and in a 3D ball (3D analogue of the Vogel spiral) are newly obtained.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.12333v8", "source": "Graiff Zurita, Kane, Oishi-Tomiyasu, Packing theory derived from phyllotaxis and products of linear forms, abstract", "date": "v1 2021-06-23; v8 2024-09-26; Constr. Approx. 60(3):515–545, online 2024-09-30, DOI 10.1007/s00365-024-09691-3", "kind": "primary (refereed)", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Example 7, pp.24–25: f(x) ∝ x3^(1/3)·(x2, √(r²−x2²) sin(2πx1/s), √(r²−x2²) cos(2πx1/s)) maps a finite box D onto a ball. The points have radii r·x3^(1/3), not an indexed k^(1/3). Cites Akiyama but not AT."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:30:    {"claim": "Angular uniform distribution of lattice directions is classical in every dimension", "quote": "It is well known that, as T → ∞, these points become uniformly distributed on S_1^{d−1}", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/0706.4395", "source": "Marklof–Strömbergsson, Annals of Math. 172 (2010) 1949–2033, §2.2, eqs. (2.7)–(2.8), p.7", "date": "arXiv v2 2008-03-31", "kind": "primary (refereed)", "verified": true, "stance": "counter", "scope": "Directions of all (affine) lattice points in B_T, counted with multiplicity. This is our eq. (2.4) at ball cutoffs. Extending to every prefix only needs the tie count to be o(N)."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:32:    {"claim": "Bounded displacement preserves density (standard)", "quote": "Let Λ1 and Λ2 be Delone sets in R^d with natural density α1 and α2 respectively, where α1 ≠ α2. Then there is no BD-map Λ1 → Λ2.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.01597v2", "source": "Frettlöh–Smilansky–Solomon, Bounded displacement non-equivalence in substitution tilings, Corollary 3.2", "date": "2020-09-07 (v2)", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "counter", "scope": "Stated for origin-centered balls. Our Jordan-set transfer (§6 of RESULTS) is the same standard argument applied to a thin strip around the boundary."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:34:    {"claim": "Later spiral literature asks about visibility, not density", "quote": "The author conjectures that there is a spiral set that is a dense forest, this is in contrast to a conjecture of Adiceam and Tsokanos in [AT22].", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet, Chabauty Limits of Fermat Spirals, Further Questions", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "No mention of a higher-dimensional density question."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:35:    {"claim": "Older lattice 'spiral dispositions' are labelings, not radius rescalings", "quote": "All four dispositions give some representations of the set of naturals in the form of a finite set of polynomials in the position coordinates of a natural number.", "url": "https://api.zbmath.org/v1/document/1249.11060", "source": "zbMATH summary of Berestovskii, Mat. Tr. 8(2):49–68 (2005)", "date": "2005; Sib. Adv. Math. translation 2006", "kind": "review summary (full text unread)", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "Square, hexagonal, quadrant and cubic lattices; primes visible from the origin. Nothing indicates placing the k-th point at radius k^(1/n). Overlap risk looks low but is not excluded."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:39:  "assumptions_checked": "I re-read AT v3 pp.1–4, 22–23 and v1 §6. Points have exact radii k^(1/n) (Eq. 1.3). Delone means all-pair separation plus ambient covering. Density must hold for every bounded measurable X with null boundary. Directions are unrestricted. Our construction meets all of these for each fixed n≥2 with no extra hypothesis: tie order is arbitrary, non-primitive vectors are kept, and the points are distinct because their radii are. Our directions repeat along rational rays; AT allow this.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:41:    {"baseline": "AT Theorem 1.1, every n≥2", "baseline_assumptions": "α∈R^(n−1) badly approximable; bi-Lipschitz lifts τ1, τ2 satisfying (3.1) (stereographic composed with the cube-to-ball map s); even/odd index split.", "baseline_conclusion": "S = {k^(1/n) u_k} is Delone and effectively constructible. No density claim. [INFERENCE] It has none: the lifts do not preserve measure, so the directions are not uniformly distributed.", "ours_assumptions": "None beyond fixed n≥2.", "ours_conclusion": "Delone; ‖s_k − V_n^(1/n) z_k‖ = O(|z_k|^(−(n−1)/(n+1))); density λ(X)/V_n for every bounded Jordan X; directions uniformly distributed on caps.", "delta": "Adds the density and uniform-distribution property AT left open. Replaces their dynamical lift with a static lattice ranking. Gives the extra structure 'vanishing perturbation of a scaled lattice'. Loses: not an orbit, directions not distinct, no numerical Delone constants."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:43:    {"baseline": "AT §6 question", "baseline_assumptions": "Exact radii; arbitrary directions; n≥3.", "baseline_conclusion": "Open: does a Delone spiral with density V_n^(−1) exist?", "ours_assumptions": "As stated.", "ours_conclusion": "Yes, explicitly, for every n≥2.", "delta": "A direct answer to the exact question as posed."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:47:  "exact_novelty_delta": "This is the first written explicit construction found in this bounded screen that answers AT §6: s_k = k^(1/n) z_k/|z_k|, with z_k running through Z^n∖{0} in order of length. The contribution is the observation that ranking any lattice by length and placing the k-th point at radius k^(1/n) moves each point by o(1), because the ball-count error is o(R^(n−1)). Delone, density and uniform distribution then follow from standard facts. Priority is not established: search misses do not prove novelty, and the HDR PDF and Berestovskii full text are unread.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:50:    "explanation": "It does answer an explicit question in a refereed paper's arXiv versions, apparently still standing (journal retention inferred). That gives it some specialist value: it shows the density requirement is no obstacle once directions are unconstrained. However, the answer only rearranges classical lemmas that all predate the question: lattice-ball error bounds, the 'well known' uniform distribution of lattice directions, and density transfer under bounded displacement. Shell-numbering lattice points was floated in 2010 in the very MathOverflow thread AT cite. It plausibly merits a short note. It is not a substantive specialist result. It is not a packing, energy or phyllotaxis result, and it is not a breakthrough. It also leaves open the questions the motivating authors care about: orbit-type constructions (Akiyama, AT §6, GZ-K-OT's global problem)."
evidence/DensitySota.md:52:  "regular_vs_dynamical": "Regular constructions with arbitrary directions (ours) settle the existence question as literally posed. Fixed-angle or dynamical constructions are a different matter: Kronecker/toral lifts (AT Thm 1.1), polyhedral flows (AT Thm 5.1), transitive-map orbits (Akiyama §4), golden-angle generalizations (Lodkin; GZ-K-OT). None of these is known to have asymptotic density for n≥3. Our result says nothing about them.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:54:    "The journal text of §6 is unverified. If the referee removed the question, the claim weakens to 'stated in arXiv v1–v3'.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:55:    "Experts may regard the answer as folklore: Marklof–Strömbergsson call the direction equidistribution 'well known', and the 2010 MathOverflow answer suggested shell-numbering.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:56:    "[INFERENCE, unverified, not found in literature] AT's own Propositions 3.1 and 4.1 may answer n=3 dynamically. One would swap in equal-area bi-Lipschitz lifts, e.g. an area-preserving concentric square-to-disk map composed with Lambert azimuthal equal-area projection. The question may not be deep even inside AT's framework.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:61:    "For n≥3, does a fixed-angle or dynamical spiral Delone set with density V_n^(−1) exist? (Akiyama's transitive map; Kronecker lifts with equal-volume bi-Lipschitz maps.)",
evidence/DensitySota.md:68:    "arXiv v1 already posed the question; v3 is confirmed as the latest version on the live listing.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:69:    "Crossref journal references (deposited 2026-10-07) match v3, including Drmota–Tichy, cited only in §6: indirect evidence that §6 was kept.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:72:    "MathOverflow 24850 (AT's ref [10]) read in full: an informal 2010 precursor (user7234) and dynamical suggestions (Carnahan); no proof.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:75:    "Marklof–Strömbergsson 2010 (2.8) and El-Baz–Marklof–Vinogradov 2013: angular uniform distribution of lattice directions is classical.",
evidence/DensitySota.md:83:    {"claim": "HDR content", "url": "https://hal.science/tel-04263222v1/file/HDR.pdf", "reason": "The /file, /document, /preview and theses.hal.science routes all returned a 12.2 KB HTML page instead of the PDF. Only full-text index probes were possible."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:87:    {"claim": "Adiceam talk on the paper", "url": "https://disk.yandex.ru/i/W1xTnSGSDGWdqA", "reason": "A 67 MB mp4 from 2020-12-01, before v3. Not inspected."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:88:    {"claim": "Chazelle 'orbital construction for points on a sphere'", "url": "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/24850", "reason": "Book reference from Dehaye's answer (2010-09-27); book not read; finite-point-set context."},
evidence/DensitySota.md:91:    {"claim": "Semantic Scholar citation list", "url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/DOI:10.7169/facm/1958", "reason": "Fetch tool rejected the URL format."},
evidence/ForestSota.md:6:  "summary": "Both open statements were re-verified in the latest versions: Tevet's existence conjecture is in arXiv v1 (2025-06-28) and v2 (2025-09-16), and the Adiceam–Tsokanos (AT22) nonexistence conjecture survives the referee-revised v2 (2022-06-03, p.6). The publisher full texts are paywalled. Indirect evidence that Tevet's published version keeps the conjecture: Crossref lists Bishop 2011 among its references, and arXiv v2 cites Bishop only in the Further Questions section. A 2025 University of Toronto PhD thesis (Kashkan) independently says spiral-forest existence is 'still unknown'. Citation sweeps (OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar, Crossref), an arXiv math 'dense forest' sweep, an OpenAlex full-text sweep for 2022–2026, and Adiceam's arXiv listing through Sept 2026 found nothing that settles the question. Tevet's own Proposition 6 (a set is a dense forest iff none of its limits under translation to infinity has an empty strip) gives a published route to the package's stability step [INFERENCE]. The best known visibility bounds in the plane are O(eps^-1 ln eps^-1) (Kashkan, probabilistic) and O(eps^-1 ln eps^-1 (ln ln eps^-1)^(1+eta)) (Tsokanos, deterministic). The optimal O(1/eps) is equivalent to Danzer's problem and remains open.",
evidence/ForestSota.md:13:    "AT22 latest is v2 (2022-06-03, comment 'Updated with comments from the referee'); STATUS and provenance.json pin v1. The open-question and nonexistence-conjecture passage and Definition 1.4 are unchanged in v2 (p.6 and p.3).",
evidence/ForestSota.md:14:    "AT22 journal milestones (publisher landing page): received 2021-10-31, revised 2022-06-03 (same date as arXiv v2), accepted 2022-06-17, published 2022-08-13. The publisher PDF returned 'PDF Access Denied' today; it was not bypassed.",
evidence/ForestSota.md:15:    "Tevet arXiv v1 (2025-06-28) already contained the identical Further Questions conjecture.",
evidence/ForestSota.md:16:    "Crossref record for Tevet's journal version (indexed 2025-11-20): reference list includes Bishop_2011 (DOI 10.4310/PAMQ.2011.v7.n1.a7); 'is-referenced-by-count' is 0. In arXiv v2, Bishop is cited only in Further Questions (line 631 of the pinned text). This is indirect evidence that the published version keeps the conjecture paragraph [INFERENCE].",
evidence/ForestSota.md:25:    "Visibility bounds (SOTA) re-verified from primary sources: Alon 2018 Thm 1.1; Tsokanos 2022 Cor 1.0.1, including his doubt about Alon's sketched improvement; Kashkan v3 Thm 1.1; ASW Thms 1.3–1.4 and the Peres forest at O(eps^-3); Shirandami Thms 1.1–1.2; the Danzer equivalence (AT22 p.4; Alon §3).",
evidence/ForestSota.md:26:    "Kashkan's latest arXiv version is v3 (2024-12-16); STATUS and late-source-checks cite v2.",
evidence/ForestSota.md:30:    {"claim": "Tevet explicitly conjectures that a spiral dense forest exists, contrary to AT22.", "quote": "The author conjectures that there is a spiral set that is a dense forest, this is in contrast to a conjecture of Adiceam and Tsokanos in [AT22].", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet, Chabauty Limits of Fermat Spirals, arXiv v2, Further Questions, PDF p.12 (pinned source/tevet2025v2.txt:626-628)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary paper (arXiv, post-acceptance version)", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "planar spiral sets {sqrt(n) u_n}, arbitrary u_n"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:31:    {"claim": "The same conjecture already appears in arXiv v1.", "quote": "The author conjectures that there is a spiral set that is a dense forest, this is in contrast to a conjecture of Adiceam and Tsokanos in [AT22].", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v1", "source": "Tevet arXiv v1, Further Questions", "date": "2025-06-28", "kind": "primary paper (arXiv v1)", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "version persistence"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:32:    {"claim": "Tevet's definitions use arbitrary directions with no Delone condition; dense forest means finite density plus a decreasing visibility function V with every eps×V(eps) rectangle hit.", "quote": "there is a decreasing function V : (0, ∞) → (0, ∞), called a visibility function, such that for every ε > 0, every ε×V(ε) rectangle intersects X.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet v2 §1, p.1 (pinned :21-40)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary definition", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "target definition; the package theorem (RESULTS §§1,6) matches it"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:33:    {"claim": "Tevet Prop 6: X is a dense forest iff no Chabauty limit (limit of translates T_n X with T_n(0)→∞) has an empty strip or half-strip. Hence perturbations that vanish at infinity preserve the dense-forest property [INFERENCE].", "quote": "For a set X ⊆ R^d, the following are equivalent: 1. X is not a dense forest; 2. X has a Chabauty limit with an infinite strip with no points", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet v2 §2, Prop 6, pp.3–4 (pinned :55-69, :145-168)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary theorem", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "known general tool; makes the package's transfer step routine"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:34:    {"claim": "Tevet's own suggested route is to build a spiral whose Chabauty limits look like Bishop/Peres lattice-union forests; the package's construction is a direct realization of it.", "quote": "if all of the Chabauty limits of a set are dense forests, the set is also a dense forest (by Proposition 6), and then constructing a spiral set whose limits are similar to the construction given by Bishop", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet v2 Further Questions p.12 (pinned :628-632)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary remark", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "explains why the answer is technically modest"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:35:    {"claim": "Tevet separates the best visibility function and a Danzer spiral set as further questions; existence alone does not answer them.", "quote": "If there are indeed spiral sets that are dense forests, one might ask what is the best visibility function, and in particular whether there is a Danzer spiral set.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet v2 p.12 (pinned :632-634)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary remark", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "boundary of the claim"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:36:    {"claim": "The conjecture came from an M.Sc. thesis in Barak Weiss's group.", "quote": "This paper is part of the Author's M.Sc. thesis at Tel Aviv University, conducted under the supervision of Barak Weiss.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.22863v2", "source": "Tevet v2 Acknowledgement (pinned :646-648)", "date": "2025-09-16", "kind": "primary acknowledgement", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "open status within a leading dense-forest group in 2025"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:38:    {"claim": "The published version's reference list still includes Bishop 2011, which arXiv v2 cites only in Further Questions, so that paragraph probably survives [INFERENCE]; zero Crossref citations.", "quote": "\"key\": \"Bishop_2011\", \"DOI\": \"10.4310/PAMQ.2011.v7.n1.a7\" … \"is-referenced-by-count\": 0", "url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.2140/cnt.2025.14.239", "source": "Crossref API record (indexed 2025-11-20)", "date": "2026-10-14 accessed", "kind": "index metadata", "verified": "partial (metadata verified; version-of-record content inferred)", "stance": "supports", "scope": "published version likely retains the conjecture"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:39:    {"claim": "AT22's referee-revised v2 keeps the open-question statement and the nonexistence conjecture.", "quote": "By contrast, the existence of a spiral set which is a dense forest remains an open question. As a matter of fact, the authors conjecture that there is no such set but have not been able to establish this claim.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.01843v2", "source": "Adiceam–Tsokanos, Visibility Properties of Spiral Sets, arXiv v2 p.6 (after Fig.1)", "date": "2022-06-03", "kind": "primary paper (latest arXiv)", "verified": true, "stance": "counter", "scope": "contrary conjecture for spirals {n^(1/(d+1)) u_n} in R^(d+1), d≥1, including the plane; refuted for d=1 if the package proof holds"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:40:    {"claim": "AT22 v2 is the referee-updated latest version; journal revision date matches.", "quote": "Updated with comments from the referee … Received: 31 October 2021 Revised: 3 June 2022 Accepted: 17 June 2022 Published: 13 August 2022", "url": "http://export.arxiv.org/api/query?id_list=2111.01843,2506.22863,2407.01633 ; https://msp.org/cnt/2022/11-2/moscow-v11-n2-p02-p.pdf", "source": "arXiv API plus MSP landing page (publisher PDF access denied)", "date": "2026-10-14 accessed", "kind": "version and publisher metadata", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "version currency"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:41:    {"claim": "AT22 Thm 1.6(4) gives only an iff characterization (a moving and shrinking target condition), not existence.", "quote": "The spiral S_d(U) is a dense forest with visibility function W for some constants c_U ≥ 1 and κ_U > 0 if and only if there exists a constant c > 0 such that", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.01843v2", "source": "AT22 v2 Theorem 1.6(4), pp.5–6", "date": "2022-06-03", "kind": "primary theorem", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "not constructive"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:42:    {"claim": "Danzer link: in the plane, a finite-density dense forest with O(1/eps) visibility is equivalent to Danzer's problem.", "quote": "a set solution to the Danzer problem in R^{d+1} is a dense forest with visibility V(ε) = C·ε^{−d} … and the converse holds true in the planar case d = 1.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.01843v2", "source": "AT22 v2 p.4", "date": "2022-06-03", "kind": "primary statement", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "a spiral forest without O(1/eps) visibility says nothing about Danzer"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:44:    {"claim": "Kashkan Thm 1.1 (arXiv latest v3): there is a dense forest in R^d with V(eps) in O(eps^-(d-1) ln eps^-1), and the optimal bound is open.", "quote": "It is not known if this lower bound is attainable by a dense forest.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2407.01633v3", "source": "Kashkan arXiv v3 §1, Theorem 1.1", "date": "2024-12-16", "kind": "primary paper", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "best known visibility bound; Danzer open"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:46:    {"claim": "Tsokanos Cor 1.0.1: deterministic O(eps^-(d-1) ln eps^-1 (ln ln eps^-1)^(1+eta)); he doubts Alon's sketched O(eps^-1 ln ln ln) improvement.", "quote": "the author could not verify this claim and further discussions with Prof. Alon confirm that its validity is doubtful.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2111.02773v1", "source": "Tsokanos arXiv v1 pp.2–3 (Mathematika 68(4) 2022, DOI 10.1112/mtk.12153)", "date": "2021-11-04", "kind": "primary paper", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "best deterministic visibility bound"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:47:    {"claim": "ASW: explicit lattice-union forests: the Peres forest at O(eps^-3) and a uniformly discrete three-grid forest at O(eps^-(5+eta)) (Thm 1.3).", "quote": "There is a union of three translated lattices in R2 which is a uniformly discrete dense forest with visibility bound v(ε) = O(ε^{−(5+η)}) for any η > 0.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1907.03501v2", "source": "Adiceam–Solomon–Weiss, JLMS 2022, Thm 1.3 and p.3 (pinned :44-104)", "date": "2021-05-26", "kind": "primary paper", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "explicit-rate bases the same rank trick could reuse [INFERENCE]"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:48:    {"claim": "Shirandami Thm 1.1: unions of grids (all translates) are dense forests iff an irrationality condition holds; two grids suffice in the plane; compactness gives no rate.", "quote": "The compactness argument at the end of the proof is a crucial step that precludes an explicit visibility bound.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2303.14719v2", "source": "Shirandami, Math. Z. 2023, Thm 1.1 and §2 (pinned :143-161, :430-433)", "date": "2023", "kind": "primary theorem", "verified": true, "stance": "supports", "scope": "the base forest is prior art; explains why the package has no explicit rate"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:49:    {"claim": "The Chabauty / orbit-closure viewpoint is standard in the Weiss school.", "quote": "The collection C of closed subsets of R^d is compact with respect to a natural topology sometimes referred to as the Chabauty topology.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1406.3807v2", "source": "Solomon–Weiss, Dense forests and Danzer sets, §7.3", "date": "2014-07-10", "kind": "primary paper", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "the o(1) transfer is routine for specialists [INFERENCE]"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:50:    {"claim": "The same authors did not use norm-ranking even for spiral Delone sets; they left a density question that norm-ranking would address.", "quote": "It is not clear to the authors whether a spiral Delone set with asymptotic density r = V_n^{-1} can exists in any dimension n ≥ 3.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "Adiceam–Tsokanos, Higher Dimensional Spiral Delone Sets, v3 §6 (pinned :1082-1083)", "date": "2021-03-30", "kind": "primary paper", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "consistent with the rank trick being overlooked in this literature"},
evidence/ForestSota.md:51:    {"claim": "The literature itself warns that 'spiral' sets need not look spiral; the literal definition allows the package's non-phyllotactic set.", "quote": "the terminology “spiral” sets … can be slightly misleading : … for various choices of the real sequence x, the set S_x need not resemble a spiral.", "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.06428v3", "source": "Adiceam–Tsokanos Delone v3, footnote 1, p.2", "date": "2021-03-30", "kind": "primary remark", "verified": true, "stance": "nuance", "scope": "definitional breadth"},
