{
  "raw_result": "`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- Several \"scope limits\" in the draft are really impossibility results. A fixed badly approximable (BA) angle has a lower bound √(2πc) on the shortest vector of every limit lattice, and no Fermat spiral is a dense forest. Calling an unavoidable restriction a \"limitation\" undersells a theorem that is already in its best possible form.\n- Forest and density use the same mechanism: sort a lattice-like set by norm, and a lattice-count error of o(R^{d−1}) makes the radial correction vanish. Their priority risk is one shared bet, not two separate ones.\n- Contradicting an expert's published conjecture is the cheapest strong sign of significance available. It is only as good as the verbatim quote behind it.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"ready-with-fixes\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"target\": \"all (bottom_line)\",\n      \"problem\": \"SUBJECTIVE+FACTUAL: The bottom line answers mainly by negation ('not established', 'mathematical refinements', 'may be meaningful'). It never defines 'major', so the negative tells the user little, and it does not say which results matter or why. 'Refinements' understates complete answers to explicitly posed questions, one of which contradicts a published conjecture.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"assessment-draft.json bottom_line; packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/STATUS.md §1; packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/STATUS.md 'Exact open statement'; packages/phys-spiral-delone-uniform-density/STATUS.md 'Explicit primary open statement'.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Define 'major' (new method, long-standing or prominent problem, quantitative state-of-the-art gain, physical result); none of the five qualifies. Then say positively what does exist, conditional on human review and priority checks. See defensible_bottom_line.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"soliton (bottom_line, physical-context)\",\n      \"problem\": \"FACTUAL: The cone-energy disclaimer is applied to all five packages. The soliton target's book bridge is Lou–Li's integrable-models chapter, not Cao's cone chapter, and the supplied physical baseline covers cones only.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"assessment-draft.json bottom_line; physical-context.json comparison_inference ('Our five packages...'); packages/phys-supersoliton-degree-obstruction/README.md 'Book bridge' (pp.350–351), vs Cao pp.541–543 in the other four READMEs.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Restrict the cone-energy sentence to the four phyllotaxis-geometry targets. Compare the soliton result against Wang–Tang–Liang 2018 and Delisle–Jaouadi 2026 (soliton STATUS.md); no physics baseline was supplied for it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"target\": \"forest\",\n      \"problem\": \"SUBJECTIVE emphasis + EVIDENCE GAP: Forest is mildly understated. The assessment measures it against the Danzer problem and quantitative visibility rates, which are different problems. It does not use the result's strongest signal: it goes against Adiceam–Tsokanos's published nonexistence conjecture. Adiceam had already co-authored the lattice-union forest paper (arXiv:1907.03501) when that conjecture appeared (arXiv:2111.01843), which suggests the norm-ranking step was not obvious to specialists. However, the snapshot quotes only the 'remains an open question' sentence and paraphrases the conjecture, and parent-evidence.json did not recheck that source. Tevet later proved the Fermat case negative (Cor. 7), and the construction (a perturbed union of two irrationally rotated lattices) is not uniformly discrete. The conjecture's exact scope therefore decides whether 'refutes' is accurate.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/STATUS.md §1 (quote + paraphrase) and §3 table (Adiceam–Solomon–Weiss; Shirandami); packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/RESULTS.md §1 Theorem (no uniform discreteness); packages/phys-spiral-delone-uniform-density/STATUS.md forward-screen Tevet row ('No Fermat spiral is a dense forest'); assessment-draft.json targets[0].assessment; parent-evidence.json has no Adiceam–Tsokanos entry.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Pin the verbatim 2022 conjecture sentence and its definitions (class of spiral sets, dense-forest definition, any separation requirement). If they match, write: 'settles an explicitly open existence question affirmatively, confirming Tevet 2025 and contradicting Adiceam–Tsokanos 2022.' Keep Danzer, visibility rate and Delone as scope notes, not as the measuring stick.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"target\": \"forest + density\",\n      \"problem\": \"FACTUAL omission + EVIDENCE GAP: The forest and density priority risks are linked, but the draft treats them separately. The unresolved Berestovskii 2005 lead (full text never inspected) appears only under density, although forest STATUS lists it too and the forest proof follows density's rank/discrepancy argument. Density's sources are also thinner than those for the 2025 questions: the question dates from 2021, the published FACM 2022 text was not inspected, Adiceam's 2023 HDR was not obtained, Google Scholar returned 403, and only an OpenAlex first-hop citation list was screened. One earlier appearance of the norm-ranking + o(1)-displacement idea would undercut both targets. (The late-2021 'remains open' forest statement is weak circumstantial evidence that the authors did not have this route then; it says nothing about 2022–2026.)\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/STATUS.md §3 final paragraph (density RESULTS §§3–4 'read for the general rank/discrepancy argument') and §4 Berestovskii row; packages/phys-spiral-delone-uniform-density/STATUS.md 'Publication/version distinction', 'Forward-citation screen', 'Earlier-construction check'; assessment-draft.json targets[0].scope_limit vs targets[2].scope_limit.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Add the Berestovskii lead and the shared-mechanism risk to the forest scope_limit. Run forest and density as one priority check, starting with the FACM final text, the 2023 HDR, broader forward citations of both Adiceam–Tsokanos papers, and Berestovskii. These sources bear on both targets.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"universality + forest\",\n      \"problem\": \"FACTUAL framing: Some scope_limits list impossible strengthenings as if they were open gaps. (i) 'Not one BA angle realizing all lattice shapes, no target-independent digit bound': for a fixed BA α with constant c, every limit lattice has shortest vector ≥ √(2πc), and continued-fraction digits ≤ M force c ≥ 1/(4(M+1)). Very thin targets therefore force both α and M to depend on the target, so 'for each Λ, some α' is the best possible form and matches Tevet's 'some Fermat spiral'. (ii) 'Not a constant-angle Fermat forest': impossible by Tevet Cor. 7. (iii) 'No explicit optimal visibility rate': there is no explicit rate at all, because the visibility function comes from a compactness argument.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-fermat-chabauty-classification/RESULTS.md §8 (|v|²≥2πc; 'already known from Tevet's Corollary 5'); packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/RESULTS.md §1 bridge and §7; packages/phys-spiral-delone-uniform-density/STATUS.md Tevet row; packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/RESULTS.md §1 ('uses compactness; it does not supply an explicit numerical visibility rate'); assessment-draft.json targets[0..1].scope_limit.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Relabel (i) and (ii) as restrictions that are provably necessary, not limitations. Change (iii) to 'no explicit visibility rate (obtained by compactness)'.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"medium\",\n      \"target\": \"universality\",\n      \"problem\": \"SIGNIFICANCE CALIBRATION / EVIDENCE GAP: The draft rightly does not call universality an elementary corollary. The proof uses one non-elementary published result (McMullen's absolute-winning theorem with quasisymmetric invariance), which makes two ratios linked by a Möbius map badly approximable at the same time. It then builds the angle explicitly by concatenating bounded-digit continued-fraction blocks with parity padding. Referee inference: a fixed BA angle only has bounded-digit convergent data (Tevet Thm 2 / RESULTS §3), so the real difficulty is choosing a normalization in which both ratios are BA. Geometrically, that means finding a geodesic through a prescribed point whose two endpoints are both BA, then concatenating symbolically. A dynamics specialist could read the result as a translation of known bounded-geodesic/winning-set facts through Bacher's geodesic picture. Parent-evidence checked only forward-bounded statements (Guan–Wu) and correctly declined to equate them with what is needed. Nobody has searched for the two-sided statement.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/RESULTS.md §§2–3 (u=h²/(β−x)−x; Bad∩G⁻¹(Bad); blocks (2)); STATUS.md baseline table (Bacher Prop. 6.1; McMullen Thm 1.3/2.2); parent-evidence.json bacher.facts (geodesic/modular framework is prior art), mcmullen.our_comparison_inference, guan-wu-new.limit.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Describe the delta as 'a complete positive answer using one published non-elementary input; not elementary, not a new method'. Have the specialist check whether the two-sided bounded-geodesic statement is already in the literature. If it is, credit it: the contribution becomes the translation plus a definitive answer to Tevet's question. That is still an answer, but with lower novelty.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"universality + classification\",\n      \"problem\": \"EVIDENCE GAP: Two texts relevant to priority are effectively unread. (a) The 2025 book 'Mathematical Phyllotaxis', which sits squarely in this area: only the abstract and metadata of chapter 5 (Swinton) were seen, and other chapters may treat Vogel/Fermat local lattices. (b) The published CNT version of Tevet: its Further Questions wording, and whether the Thm 2 display was corrected.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"parent-evidence.json swinton-new.read_scope ('Publisher abstract and metadata only') and .limit; tevet-journal.limit; packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/STATUS.md 'Source caution'.\",\n      \"correction\": \"List both as open priority checks. Do not let one chapter abstract stand in for the whole book.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"universality + classification (anti_hype_checks)\",\n      \"problem\": \"FACTUAL: The anti-hype check names only the shared radial-ranking mechanism. Universality and classification also share Bacher's model and the same local inclusion lemmas (identical constants 5R²/r and 45R²/r, developed jointly).\",\n      \"evidence\": \"assessment-draft.json anti_hype_checks[4]; packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/RESULTS.md §5 ('independently developed with the companion classification worker'; bounds (8)–(9)); packages/phys-fermat-chabauty-classification/RESULTS.md Lemmas 2.1–2.2.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Present the work as three items: (A) norm-ranking spiral constructions (forest + density); (B) Fermat Chabauty limits (universality, with classification as a supporting proposition crediting Bacher); (C) the scoped soliton no-go.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"soliton\",\n      \"problem\": \"UNSUPPORTED wording: 'possibly a useful correction of an overoptimistic test' credits the 2018 paper with a claim it did not make. It proposed the three-super-soliton test as a route and never said the system passes it. What the result shows is that the route fails for n≥4 under a generic F–F–G reading that we chose ourselves, checked against Φ₂=Φ₃=0. Odd-n polarized sectors (b=±a, including F=G) remain open. Constrained bosonic n=5 F=G three- and four-soliton families are already known (Wang–Yang–Tang–Chen 2024). The bosonic core may also overlap the Hietarinta-type classifications that STATUS names but did not inspect.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-supersoliton-degree-obstruction/STATUS.md (2018 quotes; Wang–Yang–Tang–Chen 2024 row; Hietarinta sentence); RESULTS.md §1.2, §3.3 Lemma 3.1, §4; assessment-draft.json targets[4].assessment.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Reword as: 'evidence against the suggested three-super-soliton route for n≥4 under a stated generic mixed-seed interpretation, calibrated at n=2,3; restricted sectors and the symmetry hypothesis untouched.' Inspect the Hietarinta-type sources before making any external claim.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"low\",\n      \"target\": \"all\",\n      \"problem\": \"EVIDENCE STRENGTH not recorded: All correctness evidence comes from AI model reviewers. Forest and universality each had reviews from two vendors (Claude + Codex). Density, classification and soliton each had a single claude-opus-5-5 review, the same model family as this referee, so their blind spots may overlap. No human has reviewed any target.\",\n      \"evidence\": \"packages/phys-spiral-dense-forest/RESULTS.md §10; packages/phys-fermat-lattice-universality/RESULTS.md §9; packages/phys-spiral-delone-uniform-density/RESULTS.md 'Completed independent review'; packages/phys-fermat-chabauty-classification/RESULTS.md §10; packages/phys-supersoliton-degree-obstruction/RESULTS.md §7.\",\n      \"correction\": \"Record the review status for each target. Keep 'if arguments survive human review'. Never present the package labels (SOLVED/DELIVERY-READY/Certification) to the user as outside acceptance.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"per_target_assessment\": {\n    \"forest\": {\n      \"delta_vs_baseline\": \"Proves that some spiral set {√k·u_k} with arbitrary directions is a dense forest under Tevet's definition (origin-ball finite density is automatic for exact radii). The construction sorts the already-known forest Z²∪QZ² (ASW 2022; Shirandami 2023) by norm. The classical O(R^{2/3}) error in the circle lattice-point count makes the radial correction O(r^{-1/3}), and visibility survives a displacement that tends to zero. It is not a Fermat spiral (impossible), not uniformly discrete, and has no explicit visibility rate.\",\n      \"referee_significance\": \"SUBJECTIVE: top tier of the five. Two published sources conjectured opposite answers, so the result was not obvious to specialists (pending the verbatim quote). The depth is low: a short transfer argument from known ingredients. It also shows the exact-radius constraint costs nothing once the radial counting error is o(R) (referee inference). It does not improve dense-forest benchmarks.\",\n      \"draft_calibration\": \"Slightly understated on significance; the scope list is incomplete (Berestovskii lead; shared-mechanism risk).\",\n      \"correctness_evidence\": \"Written proof; two model reviews (MINOR-REVISION, no mathematical defect); no human review.\",\n      \"open_checks\": \"Verbatim 2022 conjecture and definitions; Berestovskii full text; the density-side sources; a dense-forest specialist's view on whether 'spiralizing' a lattice-union forest is folklore.\"\n    },\n    \"universality\": {\n      \"delta_vs_baseline\": \"For each covolume-π lattice Λ, there is one BA angle α (depending on Λ's shape) such that every rotation and translate of Λ is a translation-only Chabauty limit of X_α. This is exactly Tevet's quantifier plus freedom in orientation and translation; the dependence of α and of the digit bound on the target is unavoidable. Prior work supplies Bacher's local model and area π, and McMullen's winning-set theorems. New here: choosing a point on a Möbius curve where both ratios are BA, the parity-padded bounded-digit concatenation, unimodular convergent bases, and sublinear index windows.\",\n      \"referee_significance\": \"SUBJECTIVE: top tier. A complete answer to an explicit 2025 question, using one genuinely non-elementary input. It is not an elementary corollary (the draft does not call it one) and not a new method; specialists may see it as a translation of known bounded-geodesic facts.\",\n      \"draft_calibration\": \"About right. Relabel the unavoidable 'limits' as necessary restrictions, and add the geodesic-level priority check.\",\n      \"correctness_evidence\": \"Claude CORRECT, Codex CORRECT-WITH-FIXES. The finite numerical check skips the winning-set step (RESULTS §8). No human review.\",\n      \"open_checks\": \"Whether the two-sided bounded-geodesic statement is already in the literature; the CNT published text; the rest of the 2025 'Mathematical Phyllotaxis' book.\"\n    },\n    \"density\": {\n      \"delta_vs_baseline\": \"For every n≥3, the construction (Z^n sorted by norm) gives a spiral Delone set s_k=k^{1/n}u_k with density λ(X)/V_n for every bounded Jordan-measurable X. That is the source's actual definition (p.22). Counting points in origin-centred balls is automatic (⌊R^n⌋) and is not the content. Angular equidistribution follows. Spiral Delone sets alone already existed (Adiceam–Tsokanos's toral lifting). The construction also covers n=2, which was not the open case.\",\n      \"referee_significance\": \"SUBJECTIVE: a literal answer to a question the source authors said was unclear to them, via a short classical argument with the same mechanism as forest. Companion-level; best presented alongside forest.\",\n      \"draft_calibration\": \"Significance about right; priority risk understated.\",\n      \"correctness_evidence\": \"Single model review ('correct'); no human review.\",\n      \"open_checks\": \"FACM 2022 final text (was the question kept or answered?); Adiceam's 2023 HDR; broader forward citations; Berestovskii.\"\n    },\n    \"classification\": {\n      \"delta_vs_baseline\": \"For every real α, every nonempty translation limit is an affine line or an affine lattice of covolume π. For each irrational α that is not BA, the empty set, every line and at least one lattice all occur. This sharpens Tevet's p.4 containment by dropping Z×R, and contradicts the p.12 computational suggestion that Z×R occurs. The covolume-π answer is already implied in substance by Bacher Thm 1.1 + Rem. 3.3, which Tevet's bibliography does not cite.\",\n      \"referee_significance\": \"SUBJECTIVE: a supporting result. It is properly treated as heavily overlapping Bacher 2014 rather than as a new local mechanism. Ruling out Z×R is a small but definite correction of a published computational suggestion.\",\n      \"draft_calibration\": \"Correct.\",\n      \"correctness_evidence\": \"Single-vendor review (CORRECT-WITH-FIXES) plus follow-up review (ACCEPT); no human review.\",\n      \"open_checks\": \"Nothing beyond the universality checks. Fold into that write-up with Bacher credited first.\"\n    },\n    \"soliton\": {\n      \"delta_vs_baseline\": \"For all n≥4, the 2018 paired system has no generic F–F–G square-free three-super-soliton family, even allowing nilpotent dressings. The criterion is our own Hietarinta-type choice, checked by the fact that it vanishes at n=2,3. It does not address the source's actual hypothesis (symmetry integrability for n>3). Odd-n b=±a / F=G sectors, other ansätze, Lax pairs and symmetries all remain open.\",\n      \"referee_significance\": \"SUBJECTIVE: a narrow no-go that depends on the chosen interpretation. The step specific to supersymmetry is a routine body projection, and the bosonic core may be partly classical. Lowest priority.\",\n      \"draft_calibration\": \"About right, apart from the 'overoptimistic test' wording and the cone-energy mis-scope.\",\n      \"correctness_evidence\": \"Single model review ('correct'); no human review.\",\n      \"open_checks\": \"Hietarinta-type bosonic classifications (named in STATUS, not inspected).\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"recommended_priority\": {\n    \"order\": [\"forest\", \"universality\", \"density\", \"classification\", \"soliton\"],\n    \"subjectivity_note\": \"Ranking forest above universality is subjective: forest has the published opposite conjecture, universality has more technical content. Both belong in the top tier, and the reverse order is equally defensible. Density's standing depends on forest's because they share a mechanism.\",\n    \"assessment_of_user_plan\": \"Endorsed, with four modifications. (1) Run the forest and density priority checks together, starting with the density-side sources (FACM text, HDR, Berestovskii, broader forward citations) and the verbatim 2022 conjecture. (2) Point the universality specialist at the two-sided bounded-geodesic question, the CNT published text, and the rest of the 2025 phyllotaxis book. (3) Fold classification into universality as a proposition crediting Bacher, with the Z×R remark. (4) Keep the soliton result as a scoped, calibrated no-go, check the Hietarinta-type sources first, and never present it as nonintegrability. Human specialist review is the deciding step for every target. The draft makes no publishability or priority claims; keep it that way.\",\n    \"presentation\": \"Present two coherent contributions plus one note: (A) norm-ranking spiral constructions = forest + density; (B) Chabauty limits of Fermat spirals = universality + classification; (C) a scoped supersymmetric no-go.\"\n  },\n  \"defensible_bottom_line\": \"No, not a major contribution in the usual sense. None of the five introduces a new method, resolves a long-standing or prominent problem, improves a quantitative benchmark (for example dense-forest visibility rates), or says anything about physical energy. The cone-physics comparison is a mismatch of kind, not a performance gap, so a newer physics search would not change this. The results are not trivial either. Subject to human specialist review (so far only AI models have checked the proofs) and the priority checks still under way, the evidence supports two specialist-level contributions and a companion note. (1) Spiral dense forests exist. This settles an explicitly open question, confirming Tevet 2025's conjecture and, once the verbatim text confirms it, contradicting Adiceam–Tsokanos 2022's nonexistence conjecture. (2) Every covolume-π lattice arises as a translation-only Chabauty limit of some Fermat spiral with a badly approximable angle. This fully answers Tevet 2025's question; the angle must depend on the target, so this is the best possible form. (3) The norm-ranking idea behind (1) also answers Adiceam–Tsokanos 2021's higher-dimensional density question. The classification of limit types mostly follows from Bacher 2014, though ruling out Z×R corrects a published computational suggestion. The supersymmetric result is a no-go for one fixed reading of a 2018 test, not a proof of nonintegrability. All of these are short arguments built from classical or published ingredients, answering recent questions in a small specialist literature. Present them as two coherent contributions plus a scoped note, not five breakthroughs. Next step: specialist review of forest and universality, with the forest and density priority checks run together.\"\n}\n```\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- An explicit open question, a short classical proof, and no settling source in a limited search together support \"specialist answers\". They do not support \"major\" or \"first\". The bottom line is most useful when it names which results clear that bar and why.\n- Most of the calibration value here came from two distinctions: \"impossible\" versus \"not proved\" (the unavoidable quantifiers), and \"quoted\" versus \"paraphrased\" (the 2022 conjecture).\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`"
